    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_epidermis",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Epidermis",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Both epidermal layers",
        "margin_note": "Two marks. Name both layers, the cuticle, the uneven stomata and the cavity.",
        "lines": [
          "A vertical section of a dicot leaf shows three",
          "parts:",
          "1. Epidermis   2. Mesophyll   3. Vascular system",
          {
            "text": "1. Epidermis",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The epidermis is differentiated into the",
          "adaxial epidermis on the upper surface and the",
          "abaxial epidermis on the lower surface.",
          "Both are covered with a cuticle, which reduces",
          "transpiration.",
          "The abaxial epidermis generally bears more",
          "stomata than the adaxial epidermis.",
          "Each stoma is bounded by two bean shaped guard",
          "cells and opens into a sub-stomatal cavity."
        ],
        "why": "The two surfaces of this leaf are not alike, and the epidermis is where that shows first: the shaded lower surface loses less water, so it can afford to carry more stomata.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Using the words upper and lower only. Adaxial and abaxial are the marked terms.",
          "Saying the adaxial epidermis has no stomata at all. It has fewer, not none.",
          "Leaving out the sub-stomatal cavity."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Ad- points towards the stem, ab- points away from it: adaxial is upper, abaxial is lower."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_palisade",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Palisade parenchyma",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Palisade",
        "margin_note": "Open the mesophyll here and say that it is differentiated. That word matters.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "2. Mesophyll",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The tissue lying between the upper and the",
          "lower epidermis is the mesophyll. It has",
          "chloroplasts and carries out photosynthesis.",
          "It is differentiated into two kinds of tissue,",
          "and that is why the leaf is called",
          "dorsiventral.",
          "a) Palisade parenchyma: elongated cells placed",
          "vertically and parallel to one another, lying",
          "just below the adaxial epidermis. They are",
          "packed with chloroplasts and do most of the",
          "photosynthesis."
        ],
        "why": "The palisade cells stand on end just under the surface that faces the sun, so the light passes down the length of each cell and meets the most chloroplasts. Their position and their shape are one idea, not two.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Placing the palisade layer at the bottom. It lies below the upper epidermis.",
          "Describing the palisade cells as rounded. They are elongated and vertical."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Palisade cells stand upright and side by side, just under the upper surface."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_spongy",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Spongy parenchyma",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Spongy",
        "margin_note": "Three lines. Link the air cavities to the stomata; that link is the reasoning.",
        "lines": [
          "b) Spongy parenchyma: loosely arranged,",
          "irregularly shaped cells lying below the",
          "palisade layer and reaching the abaxial",
          "epidermis.",
          "Large air cavities lie between these cells,",
          "and they are continuous with the sub-stomatal",
          "cavity.",
          "So gases entering through the stomata reach",
          "every photosynthetic cell."
        ],
        "why": "Carbon dioxide has to travel from the stoma to the chloroplasts as a gas, and gas moves far faster through air spaces than through cells, so the loose packing of the spongy layer is what makes the leaf work.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Drawing the spongy cells packed tightly. The air spaces are the point.",
          "Saying the spongy tissue does no photosynthesis. It does some; the palisade does most."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Spongy means full of holes, and the holes join up with the sub-stomatal cavity."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_vascular",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Vascular system",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Bundles described",
        "margin_note": "Two marks. Give all three bundle words, the sheath, and which tissue is on which side.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "3. Vascular system",
            "style": "heading"
          },
          "The vascular system includes the vascular",
          "bundles seen in the veins and in the midrib.",
          "The size of a bundle depends on the size of",
          "the vein.",
          "Each vascular bundle is conjoint, collateral",
          "and closed.",
          "Each bundle is surrounded by a layer of thick",
          "walled bundle sheath cells, and bundle sheath",
          "extensions may reach both the epidermal",
          "layers.",
          "The xylem lies towards the adaxial surface and",
          "the phloem towards the abaxial surface.",
          {
            "text": "Dorsiventral: palisade above, spongy below",
            "style": "boxed"
          }
        ],
        "why": "A leaf bundle keeps the orientation it had in the stem: the leaf is an outgrowth of the stem, so the side that was inner in the stem stays upper in the leaf, which puts the xylem on top.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing open for a leaf bundle. Leaf bundles are closed.",
          "Putting the phloem on the upper side. Xylem is adaxial, phloem is abaxial.",
          "Forgetting the bundle sheath."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Xylem up, phloem down: the same order as in the stem, seen sideways."
      },
